    {
      "id": "046",
      "headline": "Poet Poe's 'Toe Show' Blows a Pro's Nose in a Rose; Crows in Rows Expose a Hose",
      "summary": "The foot-themed poetry slam was interrupted by birds and plumbing; critics loved it.",
      "fullBody": "Edgar 'Poe' Poet hosted a 'Toe Show'—a poetry slam about feet—that was interrupted when a professional ('pro') sneezed into a rose, attracting crows that arranged themselves in rows and exposed a broken hose. 'The crows in rows exposed the hose during my toe show,' Poe recited, improvising. The chaos was so aesthetically pleasing that art critics declared it 'post-structuralist performance.' The pro, a ballerina with allergies, received a standing ovation for her sneeze. The crows have been hired for Poe's next show, 'Crow Row: A Flow.' The hose remains broken but is now considered part of the set design.",
      "category": "arts",
      "imageUrl": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/factictionary/news-content/main/images/2026-07-06/046.jpg",
      "publishedAt": "1 day ago",
      "readTimeSeconds": 89,
