        "Podcast",
        "Food"
      ],
      "isBreaking": false
    },
    {
      "id": "050",
      "headline": "Warden Harden's 'Garden Pardon' Lardons a Hard-on for a Bard in a Yard; Card Fard",
      "summary": "The prison's horticulture therapy program got weird; Shakespeare is banned from the yard.",
      "fullBody": "Warden Hank Harden's 'Garden Pardon' program, where inmates grow vegetables for early release, took a strange turn when a 'bard in a yard' (Shakespearean actor doing community service) inspired a 'hard-on' for poetry among the prisoners. 'I lardoned a hard-on for a bard in a yard,' Harden explained, meaning he excused the behavior. The inmates began performing 'Romeo and Juliet' using turnips as puppets. The 'card fard' (playing card fart) incident, in which a prisoner used a trump card to make a raspberry sound during the balcony scene, caused Harden to ban Shakespeare from the yard. 'No more bards, no more cards, just carrots,' Harden ruled. The turnip puppet troupe is now underground.",
